{
  "description": "Filename analysis of spec RF-20, RF-22 and RF-23, with the naming of spec §5, as data for both implementations. Input: the path of a file relative to its entry (its folders included, separators '/' or '\\') and its kind, 'video' or 'subtitle'. Output: type ('movie', 'episode' or 'unknown'); title (dots and underscores become spaces; the year, quality, source, codec, audio, group and language tags are left out); year; season (0 for specials); chapter and lastChapter (SxxEyy, SxxEyyEzz, SxxEy-Ezz, SxxEy-zz, NxNN, NxNN-zz); part (movies only: cdN, discN, partN, ptN). For subtitles: language (ISO 639-1, with a region only where Jellyfin recognizes it), forced and sdh. A key that is absent means no information; forced and sdh appear only when true. Matching is exact. The expected object lists everything the analysis must read, so a missing key must be missing in the output too. Conservative choices, where spec §5 and RF-20 do not decide: (1) the file's own name wins over its folders; a missing title or year is taken from the nearest folder (the folder above the season folder for episodes), and the year comes only together with the title it belongs to; (2) a season folder (Season N, Temporada N, SN, Specials, Especiales; Specials is season 0) makes the file an episode, and then the file name is a chapter title unless it is only a number; (3) a file named only with a number (01, 01 - Pilot) is an episode chapter only inside a season folder, otherwise unknown; (4) 'Title - NNN' with 1 to 3 digits and no season is absolute numbering (anime, spec §8): unknown, with no chapter; (5) the names movie, film, video, main, feature and index (without a year) are not titles: the folder gives the title, and with no folder there is none; (6) the year is the last 4-digit token (1900 to 2099) after a title and before the first tag, so 'Blade.Runner.2049.1080p' reads 2049 as the year (a known limit: the user corrects it in Assign); (7) the first tag (resolution, source, codec, audio, HDR, streaming service, bit depth) ends the title, and a group after a tag goes with it; a group with no tag before it stays in the title ('Heat-RARBG'); (8) 'Part 2' with a space is title, only the joined form (part2, cd1) is a part; (9) a language name or code at the end of a video title is removed, codes must be lowercase there ('spa', 'es'); uppercase codes count only in subtitles; (10) subtitle flags: 'forced'; 'sdh', 'cc' and 'hi' mean SDH, except that a 'hi' that is the only tag is Hindi; (11) regions: 'es-MX' and the other Latin American Spanish regions become 'es-419', 'es-ES' becomes 'es', 'en-US' and 'en-GB' become 'en', 'pt-BR', 'pt-PT', 'fr-CA' and 'zh-TW' are kept, and any other region is dropped; (12) languages accept ISO 639-1 and 639-2 codes (2 and 3 letters) and English and Spanish names of common languages. Sections: videos (no folders), folders (the folders decide or complete), subtitles, unknown.",
